      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\), we start by determining the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\nFirst, we perform the division of 5 by 14:\n\\[\n5 \\div 14 = 0.\\overline{357142}\n\\]\nThis tells us that the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is a repeating decimal with the repeating block \"357142\". The length of the repeating block is 6 digits.\n\nNext, we need to find the position of the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit within the repeating block. We do this by determining the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6. This is because the repeating block \"357142\" has a length of 6, and the position of the digit can be found using the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Position} = (1314 \\mod 6)\n\\]\nCalculating the remainder:\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\nSo, \\(1314 \\mod 6 = 0\\). A remainder of 0 indicates that the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block \"357142\". The last digit of \"357142\" is 2.\n\nTherefore, the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
